The fix is committed (19a2ec34) and queued to land, but #351 is still open. The land had no verdict when this run reached its time limit; the manager lands it anyway, and the next run finishes the issue.

**The fix:** the check now looks only inside the fixture's own output, so it runs under `target/cross-test/...` instead of filtering every path out.
- **Build output:** it skips only a `target/` directory inside that output.
- **Descriptor strings:** it ignores text inside string literals, so the reserved `JWT_PAYLOAD_TYPE_LINKED` name in a protoc descriptor no longer counts as a reader.
- **Where it lives:** the check is now a small shared script, `cross-test/lib/retired-claims.sh`, which `cross-test/current-generation/run.sh` calls.

**Tests:** a new Go test, `scripts/cross_test_retired_claims_test.go`, was written first and failed before the fix. It now passes and covers:
- planted Go, Java and Rust readers under a `target/cross-test/...` path are reported;
- a reader in a `target/` directory inside the output is ignored;
- the reserved name inside Go and Java descriptor strings stays quiet, but the same name outside a string is reported.

**Checked on real generated output:**
- The record-egress fixture passes in Go, Java and Rust with the check actually running.
- In a scratch copy of the real output under `target/cross-test/`, the real `token.pb.go` descriptor (the issue's false positive) gave no hits, and a planted `GetLinked(` was reported.

**Local `just gates`:** protoc-check, soundness, test, determinism, cert-stream, stream-expiry, memberport, claims-provenance, w2s-validate, c2d-server, zero-rpc-s2s, jwk-alg, redact, trace-carry and tacplus-script all ended `rc=0`. current-generation ended `rc=2`, so the run's last line is `=== OVERALL fail=1 ===`. The cause was the gate's guard against the source tree changing mid-run, tripped by my own commit while it ran. No fixture failed before it stopped, but I did not re-run current-generation cleanly. The land re-runs the gates.

**Release:** I did not tag v2.6.2 from main, because main has taken a feature (#321) since v2.6.1. Of the release branches, only release/2.6 has this check, so the fix goes there as backport pull request #473 (draft). It says to cut the next free patch tag on release/2.6 once it merges (v2.6.5 today, unless another backport takes it first).

**Left for the next run** (all in the last checkpoint):
- read the land verdict and which gate set it ran;
- put the main commit hash in #473 and mark it ready;
- post the outcome comment and close #351.

No service handlers changed, so the trace rule doesn't apply here.
